<BaseLayout
  title="The lab"
  description="The Halden Distribution Ltd story, the lab architecture, the tools, and the honesty statement behind this portfolio."
>
  <h1>The lab</h1>

  <section>
    <h2>One simulated company for all ten projects</h2>
    <div class="problem-statement">
      <strong>Halden Distribution Ltd.</strong> (fictional): 85 staff, one head office, one
      warehouse, 12 remote sales staff. Departments: Management, Finance, HR, Sales,
      Operations/Warehouse, and IT (me). The situation I inherited: a flat network, shared admin
      passwords, no MFA, ad-hoc backups nobody has tested, assets tracked in a spreadsheet, tickets
      arriving by email and WhatsApp, and 30% of PCs on Windows 10.
    </div>
    <p>
      Every project starts from that inherited state, fixes it, and measures the before/after. The
      domain is <code>ad.halden.internal</code> — <code>.internal</code> is the top-level domain
      ICANN reserved for private use in 2024.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <p class="small muted">
      The whole environment runs as virtual machines on one physical host (Hyper-V on Windows 11 Pro,
      16 GB RAM), and it is never exposed to the internet. VMs use dynamic memory and only the ones a
      phase needs are switched on — that is what keeps ten projects inside 16 GB. In P1 everything
      sits in one flat 192.168.10.0/24 network; project P6 splits it into the firewall-enforced zones
      below.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Host</th><th>Role</th><th>RAM (start–max)</th><th>Project</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>FW01</td><td>OPNsense gateway, VPN endpoint, DHCP relay</td><td>0.5–1 GB</td><td>P1, P6</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DC01</td><td>Domain controller, DNS, DHCP (primary), PDC emulator</td><td>1–2 GB</td><td>P1+</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DC02</td><td>Domain controller, DNS, DHCP (failover partner)</td><td>1–2 GB</td><td>P1+</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FS01</td><td>File services, DFS-N, FSRM; NPS/RADIUS in P6</td><td>1–2 GB</td><td>P1, P2, P6</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LNX01</td><td>AD-joined Ubuntu app server; vulnerability scanner in P5</td><td>0.5–1 GB</td><td>P1, P5, P7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>WS01 / WS02</td><td>Windows 11 workstations (WS02 becomes the admin PAW)</td><td>2–4 GB</td><td>P1, P3, P4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>OPS01</td><td>GLPI, Uptime Kuma, BookStack in containers</td><td>1–2 GB</td><td>P9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SIEM01</td><td>Wazuh manager, indexer and dashboard</td><td>4–6 GB (only while in use)</td><td>P7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>BKP01</td><td>Backup repository — deliberately <em>not</em> domain-joined</td><td>1–2 GB</td><td>P8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CA01</td><td>Internal enterprise CA (AD CS) for 802.1X and TLS</td><td>1–2 GB</td><td>P6</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted">Network zones after P6:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>VLAN</th><th>Zone</th><th>Subnet</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
