  <section>
    <h2>Where the work stands today</h2>
    <p>
      Each project is built as a <strong>build kit</strong> first: the design document, the numbered
      and idempotent scripts for every phase, the runbooks, the business artifacts and the evidence
      plan. The build kits for all ten projects exist in the
      <a href="https://github.com/ibrahim-official/halden-it-lab">repository</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lab execution runs phase by phase after that, and <strong>that is when the numbers appear
      here</strong>. Until a phase has actually been run, its page shows "not measured" rather than a
      figure — a deliberate choice, enforced by an automated check that fails the build if a metric
      has no evidence file behind it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Tools in the lab</h2>
    <p>
      Windows Server 2025 and Windows 11 Enterprise (evaluation), Ubuntu Server 24.04, OPNsense,
      Hyper-V, Active Directory, Entra ID (trial tenant), PowerShell, Python, Ansible, WSUS,
      Greenbone Community Edition, Wazuh, Sysmon, GLPI, BookStack, Uptime Kuma, restic, MinIO with
      Object Lock, Proxmox Backup Server, PingCastle, BloodHound CE, Windows LAPS, HardeningKitty,
      Atomic Red Team, MITRE ATT&amp;CK Navigator and draw.io. Everything used is free or an
      evaluation licence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Honesty statement</h2>
    <p>
      This is a <strong>home lab</strong>: a simulated company, on my own isolated hardware, using
      evaluation licences and free software. The configurations, scripts and documents are real. The
      measurements on this site are real measurements — and where a phase has not been run yet, the
      page says <em>not measured</em> instead of showing a number. Synthetic data (such as the
      85-user HR file, the device readiness fleet and the sample vulnerability export) is labelled as
      synthetic wherever it appears.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lab is listed on my CV under <em>Projects / Home Lab</em> — never as employment experience —
      and nothing here was ever run against any network, device or account I do not own. The small
      number of scripts that simulate attacker behaviour (P3, P7) are written to refuse to run outside
      the lab and are documented defensively: what was detected and how it was fixed, not how to
      repeat it.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
